{"id":"54846288-68bf-4fc0-be74-81796ad1b64a","object":"chat.completion","created":1784820493,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lengths = case['lengths']\\n    scores = case['scores']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(lengths)\\n    \\n    def better(a, b):\\n        if a[0] > b[0]:\\n            return a\\n        if a[0] < b[0]:\\n            return b\\n        if a[1] < b[1]:\\n            return a\\n        if a[1] > b[1]:\\n            return b\\n        if a[2] < b[2]:\\n            return a\\n        return b\\n    \\n    dp = [[None] * (budget + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\\n    for c in range(budget + 1):\\n        dp[0][c] = (0.0, 0, [])\\n    \\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\\n        w = lengths[i - 1]\\n        v = scores[i - 1]\\n        for c in range(budget + 1):\\n            skip = dp[i - 1][c]\\n            if w <= c:\\n                prev = dp[i - 1][c - w]\\n                inc = (prev[0] + v, prev[1] + w, prev[2] + [i - 1])\\n                dp[i][c] = better(skip, inc)\\n            else:\\n                dp[i][c] = skip\\n    \\n    best = dp[n][budget]\\n    return {\\n        'selected_indices': best[2],\\n        'used_tokens': best[1],\\n        'summed_score': best[0]\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that takes a case dict with lengths, scores, budget, etc. The task is a budgeted sentence selection: maximize sum of scores subject to total length <= budget, select sentences in order (no reordering), can't split sentences. Need to break ties: if two selections have same sum score, prefer fewer tokens, then lexicographic source indices (i.e., the list of indices as a tuple). Return a dict with selected indices (0-based? The example doesn't specify, but likely 0-based indices), used_tokens, summed_score.\n\nDynamic programming: For each item i and capacity c, compute best score. Also need to track tie-breaking. DP[i][c] = (max_score, used_tokens, indices) or we can store the best selection. Since we need to break ties by fewer tokens then lexicographic indices, we can store tuples (score, -used_tokens, tuple(indices)) for max comparison? Actually we want max score, then if equal, min used tokens, then lexicographically smallest indices? The spec says: \"Break equal utility by fewer used tokens and then lexicographic source indices.\" That means among selections with same sum score, pick the one with fewer tokens; if still tie, pick the one whose list of indices (presumably sorted in increasing order? But they are in document order, so the list of selected indices is naturally in order. Lexicographic comparison: compare first index, if equal then second, etc. So we want the smallest indices in lexicographic order. That is equivalent to preferring earlier indices? Actually lexicographic smaller: e.g., [0,2] vs [1,2] -> [0,2] is smaller because 0<1. So we want to minimize the tuple of indices lexicographically. So we can use a tuple ( -score? Let's think: we want to maximize score, so we negate for min? Or define a custom comparator. In Python, we can store as ( -score, used_tokens, indices ) and take min over those tuples? Because for equal score, we want lower used_tokens, then lower lexicographic indices. So we can use ( -score, used_tokens, indices ) and take the tuple that is smallest. But careful: if we have multiple selections with same score, we want lower used_tokens, which is good because we want min used_tokens. Then lexicographic indices, also min. So the tuple ( -score, used_tokens, indices ) works: smaller tuple means higher score (negated makes it more negative) but wait: -score, if score is larger, -score is smaller (more negative), so we want the minimum tuple. For tie of score, used_tokens smaller is better, which gives smaller tuple. For tie, indices smaller -> smaller tuple. So we can do min over that tuple.\n\nAlternatively, we can store (score, -used_tokens, reversed?) Not straightforward. Use ( -score, used_tokens, indices ) and then min.\n\nBut we need to track indices list. DP can store tuple of (score, used_tokens, indices). For each i, we consider including or not. Standard 0/1 knap DP where items are in order. Since we need to preserve document order, we cannot reorder, but DP naturally processes in order. We'll do DP over i (first i items) and capacity c. For each state, we store the best result for selecting from first i items with total length exactly c? Or at most c? We need to maximize score under <= budget. Usually we iterate capacity from 0 to budget. DP[i][c] = best for first i items with capacity c. Recurrence: dp[i][c] = best of dp[i-1][c] (skip) and if lengths[i-1] <= c then combine dp[i-1][c - len] with include item i-1. We need to combine tuples. We can define a helper to select best of two tuples. For combine, we add score and length, and append index to indices list. But careful: indices list must be sorted in order of selection (which is natural if we append when processing items in order). So when we include item i-1 (index i-1), we take the indices from the previous state and append the new index. That maintains order.\n\nWe need to handle case where multiple selections have same score but different used tokens and indices. The DP must pick the best according to tie-breaking. We'll implement a function better(a, b) that returns True if a is better than b. a and b are tuples (score, used_tokens, indices). Criteria: higher score better; if equal, fewer used tokens better; if equal, lexicographically smaller indices better.\n\nInitialize dp as a list of lists of tuples. For capacity 0..budget, base: dp[0][c] = (0, 0, []) for all c (since no items, score 0, tokens 0, empty list). But if we allow unused capacity, that's fine. However, we need to ensure we don't exceed budget. Since we iterate capacity up to budget, and when we include item we require len <= c, the resulting used tokens will be exactly the sum of selected lengths. We also need to consider that we might not use full capacity; the best solution might have used tokens less than budget. That's fine because dp[i][budget] will have taken the best among all capacities <= budget? Actually dp[i][c] stores best for exactly capacity c? That would be wrong because we might skip capacity. Better to store best for at most capacity c. Usually we set dp[i][c] = best using first i items with total length <= c. Then recurrence: skip: dp[i-1][c] (which is already best for <= c). Include: if len <= c, then combine dp[i-1][c - len] with item, and then compare with skip. The initial dp[0][c] = (0,0,[]) for all c. This works because dp[i-1][c] already captures best for <= c. And dp[i][c] will be the best of skip or include (where include uses c-len capacity, which itself is best for <= c-len). However, we must note that when we combine, we add the item's length and score, and the indices list from dp[i-1][c-len] already has indices in order. That gives the result for exactly the current item's length added. But dp[i-1][c-len] might have used fewer tokens than c-len, but that's okay because we are using capacity up to c-len, so total used tokens after adding is <= c. So dp[i][c] becomes best for <=c. At the end, answer is dp[n][budget].\n\nBut tie-breaking: when comparing two options, we need to compare based on the final score, used tokens (actual sum of lengths), and indices. The dp entries store the actual used tokens (the sum of lengths of selected items). So when we combine, we compute new_score = prev_score + item_score, new_tokens = prev_tokens + item_len, new_indices = prev_indices + [item_index]. Then we compare with the skip option's tuple.\n\nWe need to handle large n and budget? Not given but presumably small.\n\nReturn: selected indices (list of ints), used_tokens (int), summed_score (float). The output should be a dict.\n\nImplementation: define solve(case). Extract lengths, scores,